# Error handling

- Domain and transport failures extend `AppError` (`core/errors/app-error.ts`) with a specific `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` code and a client-safe message. They do not carry an HTTP status.
- Codes are part of the API contract: never rename or reuse one. Clients branch on `error.code`, never on `message`.
- `api/http/error-mapper.ts` is the only place that maps a code to a status and writes `{ error: { code, message, details, requestId } }`. It is registered last, after `notFound()`.
- Controllers do not `try/catch` to set a status, and do not call `res.status(4xx/5xx)`. Express 5 forwards rejected promises from async handlers. No `asyncHandler`.
- Middleware that signals an error from a callback API (rate limiter, timeout) calls `next(new …Error())`.
- 5xx responses never expose the message, stack, SQL or driver details: `INTERNAL_ERROR` / `An unexpected error occurred.`. The original error is logged with `req.log.error({ err })`.
- A unique violation is translated in the domain repository (`isUniqueViolation` from `infra/db/errors.ts`, SQLSTATE 23505) into that domain's error (`EmailTakenError`). Anything else is rethrown.
- Keep `{ cause }` when wrapping a lower-level error. Do not swallow errors with an empty `catch` unless the outcome is deliberately a boolean (`verifyPassword`).
- Do not leak existence: a resource the caller may not see is a not-found code, and login failures use one code (`INVALID_CREDENTIALS`) for unknown email and wrong password.
- `UnavailableError` carries `retryAfterSeconds`; the mapper sends `Retry-After`.

